\begin{proof}
Using the subtraction formula,
\[
\sin\left(\frac{\pi}{12}\right)=\sin\left(\frac\pi4-\frac\pi6\right)
=\sin\frac\pi4\cos\frac\pi6-\cos\frac\pi4\sin\frac\pi6
=\frac{\sqrt3}{2\sqrt2}-\frac{1}{2\sqrt2}
=\frac{\sqrt3-1}{2\sqrt2}.
\]
Since \(\sqrt2\) and \(\sqrt3\) are algebraic numbers, and the algebraic numbers are closed under addition, subtraction, multiplication, and division by a nonzero algebraic number, \(\sin(\pi/12)\) is algebraic.
\end{proof}
